            <ArticleTitle>A Bio-Inspired Approach to Task Assignment of Swarm Robots in 3-D Dynamic Environments.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>974-983</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1109/TCYB.2016.2535153</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intending to mimic the operating mechanism of biological neural systems, a self organizing map-based approach to task assignment of a swarm of robots in 3-D dynamic environments is proposed in this paper. This approach integrates the advantages and characteristics of biological neural systems. It is capable of dynamically planning the paths of a swarm of robots in 3-D environments under uncertain situations, such as when some robots are presented in or broken down or when more than one robot is needed for some special task locations. A Bezier path optimizing algorithm and a parameter adjusting algorithm are integrated in this paper. It is capable of reducing the complexity of the robot navigation control and limiting the number of convergence iterations. The simulation results with different environments demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed approach.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
